  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-fd-periodic-fourier-sobolev-spaces": [
      null,
      "## Definition\n\nFor an integer $d\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, let $Q_d=\\mathbb R^d/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^d$ carry the probability measure obtained by pushing normalized Haar measure on $\\mathbb T^d=\\mathbb R^d/\\mathbb Z^d$ forward under $y\\mapsto x=2\\pi y$; in coordinates this is $(2\\pi)^{-d}dx$, and the characters are $e_k(x)=e^{ik\\cdot x}$. For $q:Q_d\\to\\mathbb R^N$ and $k\\in\\mathbb Z^d$, set $\\widehat q(k)=\\int_{Q_d}q(x)e^{-ik\\cdot x}\\,d\\mu(x)$, componentwise, so real fields have $\\widehat q(-k)=\\overline{\\widehat q(k)}$. Put $w_k=(1+|k|^2)^{1/2}$. For any $s\\in\\mathbb R$, define $H^s(Q_d;\\mathbb R^N)$ as the completion of finite real trigonometric polynomials in the norm $\\|q\\|_{H^s}^2=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^d}w_k^{2s}|\\widehat q(k)|^2$. The completion is identified with its weighted coefficient space using finite symmetric cutoffs. If $s\\ge0$, it has a unique $L^2$ representative; for $s<0$ no $L^2$ representative is asserted."
    ],
    "def-fd-projected-no-slip-stokes-space": [
      null,
      "## Definition\n\nAssume the full Axiom of Choice. Let $d\\ge2$, let $R>0$, and let $\\Omega\\subset(-R,R)^d$ be a nonempty bounded open set. Define $H_0^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^d)$ as the closure of $C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^d)$ in the real $W^{1,2}$ norm. Define $\\operatorname{div}v=0$ distributionally to mean $\\int_\\Omega v\\cdot\\nabla\\phi=0$ for every $\\phi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$, and set $V=\\{v\\in H_0^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^d):\\operatorname{div}v=0\\}$. Equip $V$ with the inherited $H^1$ inner product $\\int_\\Omega(v\\cdot w+\\nabla v:\\nabla w)$. The phrase no-slip here means membership in this closure; no trace characterization on arbitrary $\\Omega$ is part of the definition."
    ],
    "lem-fd-rectangular-test-divergence-inverse": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nLet $d\\ge1$ and let $\\Omega=\\prod_{i=1}^d(a_i,b_i)$ be a nonempty finite open rectangle. Write $D(\\Omega)=C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$ and $D_0(\\Omega)=\\{h\\in D(\\Omega):\\int_\\Omega h=0\\}$. For every compact $K\\subset\\Omega$ there is a compact $K'\\subset\\Omega$ such that a linear map $R:D_0(\\Omega)\\to D(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^d)$ sends tests supported in $K$ to fields supported in $K'$, satisfies $\\operatorname{div}(Rh)=h$, and is continuous on each fixed-support test space for the seminorms $\\sup|\\partial^\\alpha h|$."
    ],
    "lem-fd-periodic-fourier-embedding-products": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume Countable Choice. Let $d\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$ and use $H^\\sigma(Q_d)$ with the normalized measure and weights in the Definition. For every integer $j\\ge0$ and $\\sigma>j+d/2$, each scalar $q\\in H^\\sigma(Q_d)$ has a $C^j$ representative and $\\|q\\|_{C^j}\\le C_{d,\\sigma,j}\\|q\\|_{H^\\sigma}$. For every $\\sigma>d/2$, pointwise multiplication extends uniquely from trigonometric polynomials to a continuous bilinear map $H^\\sigma\\times H^\\sigma\\to H^\\sigma$ satisfying $\\|fg\\|_{H^\\sigma}\\le C_{d,\\sigma}\\|f\\|_{H^\\sigma}\\|g\\|_{H^\\sigma}$. The assertions extend componentwise to finite-dimensional vector and tensor fields."
    ],
    "def-fd-periodic-leray-pressure-maps": [
      null,
      "## Definition\n\nAssume Countable Choice and fix $d\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$. For a vector Fourier coefficient $a(k)\\in\\mathbb C^d$, define $(Pa)(0)=a(0)$ and $(Pa)(k)=P_ka(k)$ for $k\\ne0$, where $P_k=I-kk^{\\mathsf T}/|k|^2$. For a tensor field $F=(F_{ij})_{i,j=1}^d$ with coefficients in any $H^s(Q_d)$, define the scalar pressure map $Q$ by $\\widehat{QF}(0)=0$ and $\\widehat{QF}(k)=-\\sum_{i,j=1}^d k_i k_j|k|^{-2}\\widehat F_{ij}(k)$ for $k\\ne0$. These formulas define real-preserving bounded maps on every $H^s$, $s\\in\\mathbb R$; $P$ is an orthogonal projection and commutes with derivatives. With $(\\operatorname{div}F)_i=\\sum_j\\partial_jF_{ij}$, they satisfy $\\nabla QF=-(I-P)\\operatorname{div}F$ as Fourier distributions."
    ],
    "lem-fd-periodic-tame-transport-commutator": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume Countable Choice. Let $d\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, let $m\\ge s\\ge4$ be integers, let $f,g\\in C^\\infty(Q_d;\\mathbb R)$, and define $\\Lambda^m$ by $\\widehat{\\Lambda^m q}(k)=w_k^m\\widehat q(k)$. For each coordinate $j$,\n$$\\|[\\Lambda^m,f]\\partial_jg\\|_{L^2}\\le C_{d,m,s}\\bigl(\\|f\\|_{H^m}\\|g\\|_{H^s}+\\|f\\|_{H^s}\\|g\\|_{H^m}\\bigr),$$\nwhere $[\\Lambda^m,f]h=\\Lambda^m(fh)-f\\Lambda^mh$."
    ],
    "thm-fd-periodic-pressure-recovery": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume Countable Choice. Let $d\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, let $s>d/2+1$, and let $u\\in H^s(Q_d;\\mathbb R^d)$ satisfy $\\operatorname{div}u=0$ distributionally. Then $u\\otimes u\\in H^s$, the mean-zero scalar $\\pi=Q(u\\otimes u)$ belongs to $H^s$, and in $H^{s-1}$,\n$$P(u\\cdot\\nabla u)=u\\cdot\\nabla u+\\nabla\\pi.$$\nAmong mean-zero periodic distributions satisfying the identity, this pressure is unique. The formula is a torus pressure recovery result and asserts no wall pressure regularity."
    ],
    "lem-fd-zero-trace-directional-poincare": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume full AC. Let $d\\ge2$, $R>0$, and let $\\Omega\\subset(-R,R)^d$ be bounded and open. For every $v\\in H_0^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^d)$,\n$$\\|v\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}\\le2R\\|\\partial_1v\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}\\le2R\\|\\nabla v\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}.$$\nHere $H_0^1$ is the closure of compactly supported smooth fields in the Sobolev norm."
    ],
    "thm-fd-projected-stationary-stokes": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume full AC. Let $d\\ge2$, let $R>0$, let $\\Omega\\subset(-R,R)^d$ be nonempty bounded open, let $\\mu>0$, and let $f\\in L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^d)$. There is a unique $u\\in V$ such that\n$$\\mu\\int_\\Omega\\nabla u:\\nabla v=\\int_\\Omega f\\cdot v\\qquad(v\\in V),$$\nwhere $V$ is the distributionally divergence-free subspace of $H_0^1$ defined above. It satisfies $\\|\\nabla u\\|_2\\le2R\\|f\\|_2/\\mu$. This is a projected variational velocity result; it asserts no pressure, $L^2$ pressure regularity, or classical boundary regularity."
    ],
    "lem-fd-given-smooth-solutions-difference-energy": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume Countable Choice. Fix $d\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$ and a finite interval $[0,T]$. (i) If $u,v$ are already given smooth divergence-free solutions of $z_t+P(z\\cdot\\nabla z)=\\nu\\Delta z$ on $Q_d$, with the same $\\nu\\ge0$, then for $w=u-v$,\n$$\\|w(t)\\|_2^2\\le\\|w(0)\\|_2^2\\exp\\!\\left(2\\int_0^t\\|\\nabla v(\\tau)\\|_\\infty d\\tau\\right).$$\n(ii) If $q,r$ are already given smooth solutions of $z_t+\\sum_{j=1}^dA_j(z)\\partial_jz=0$ with each $A_j:\\mathbb R^N\\to\\mathbb R^{N\\times N}$ a fixed linear map into symmetric matrices, then\n$$\\|q(t)-r(t)\\|_2^2\\le\\|q(0)-r(0)\\|_2^2\\exp\\!\\left(C\\int_0^t(\\|\\nabla q\\|_\\infty+\\|\\nabla r\\|_\\infty)d\\tau\\right).$$\nIn either case, if $s\\ge1$ and both solution families have a common $H^s$ bound $M$ on $[0,T]$, then $\\|w(t)\\|_{H^{s-1}}\\le (2M)^{(s-1)/s}\\|w(0)\\|_2^{1/s}e^{\\Gamma t/(2s)}$, where $\\Gamma$ is any uniform rate in the corresponding squared $L^2$ estimate. The torus uses the dimensionless $2\\pi$-periodic convention in the Definition; all quantities in these estimates are mathematical and no physical units are assigned to $\\nu$. These are conditional estimates for already given smooth solutions and assert no existence theorem."
    ],
    "thm-fd-rectangular-no-slip-stokes-velocity-pressure": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume full AC. Let $d\\ge2$, let $\\Omega=\\prod_{i=1}^d(a_i,b_i)\\subset(-R,R)^d$ be a nonempty finite rectangle, let $\\mu>0$, and let $f\\in L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^d)$. The projected variational theorem gives a unique $u\\in V$ with $\\mu\\int\\nabla u:\\nabla v=\\int f\\cdot v$ for all $v\\in V$ and $\\|\\nabla u\\|_2\\le2R\\|f\\|_2/\\mu$. There is a distribution $p\\in H^{-1}(\\Omega):=(H_0^1(\\Omega))'$ such that $-\\mu\\Delta u+\\nabla p=f$ in $D'(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^d)$; it is unique modulo constant distributions. With the quotient norm on $H^{-1}/\\mathbb R$, the map $f\\mapsto(u,[p])$ is bounded and linear. The pressure is not asserted to lie in $L^2$ or to be smooth at rectangle corners."
    ],
